## Hello, World!

Java is compiled to **bytecode**, which runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). This makes Java programs portable — the same `.class` file runs on any machine with a JVM.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}
```

### Compile and run

```bash
javac Main.java     # compiles to Main.class (bytecode)
java Main           # runs on the JVM
```

### Anatomy

- `public class Main` — every Java file needs a class. The public class name must match the file name.
- `public static void main(String[] args)` — the entry point the JVM calls.
- `System.out.println(...)` — prints a line to standard output.

### The JVM in one line

Source → `javac` → bytecode (`.class`) → JVM → native machine code (JIT).

### Comments

```java
// single-line comment
/* multi-line
   comment */
/** Javadoc comment — documents the API */
```

### main arguments

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("args: " + args.length);
}
```

---

**Next up:** variables and data types.

## Reading Input with Scanner

`java.util.Scanner` reads typed input from standard input.

### Basic setup

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter your age: ");
        int age = scanner.nextInt();
        System.out.println("You are " + age);
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

### Common Scanner methods

| Method          | Purpose                  |
|-----------------|--------------------------|
| `nextInt()`     | Read an `int`            |
| `nextDouble()`  | Read a `double`          |
| `nextLine()`    | Read a whole line        |
| `next()`        | Read one word (token)    |
| `hasNextInt()`  | Is there another int?    |
| `hasNextLine()` | Is there another line?   |

### Mixing nextInt and nextLine

`nextInt()` leaves the newline in the buffer. A following `nextLine()` reads that leftover newline. Fix by consuming it:

```java
int n = scanner.nextInt();
scanner.nextLine();          // consume the leftover newline
String line = scanner.nextLine();
```

### Reading until end of input

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
while (sc.hasNextInt()) {
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    System.out.println("got " + n);
}
```

### Formatting output

```java
System.out.printf("pi = %.2f%n", 3.14159);   // pi = 3.14
System.out.printf("%d + %d = %d%n", 2, 3, 2 + 3);
```

`%n` is the portable newline.

---

**Next up:** operators and type conversion.

