## Conditionals: if / else if / else

Java decisions use `if` statements. The condition must be a boolean expression:

```java
int score = 85;

if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else {
    System.out.println("Keep practicing");
}
```

### The switch statement

```java
switch (day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Monday"); break;
    case 2: System.out.println("Tuesday"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Other"); break;
}
```

Modern Java supports the arrow form without `break`:

```java
switch (day) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("Tuesday");
    default -> System.out.println("Other");
}
```

### Switch as an expression

```java
String result = switch (grade) {
    case 'A' -> "Excellent";
    case 'B' -> "Good";
    default -> "Keep trying";
};
```

### Logical operators

```java
if (x > 0 && x < 10) { ... }   // AND
if (x < 0 || y < 0)  { ... }   // OR
if (!isReady)        { ... }   // NOT
```

### Common pitfall: `=` vs `==`

`if (x = 5)` does **not compile** in Java (conditions must be boolean), which protects you from a whole class of C bugs.

### Ternary operator

```java
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
```

---

**Next up:** loops — `while`, `for`, and `do-while`.

## Loops: for, while, do-while

### for

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

### Enhanced for

```java
int[] values = {10, 20, 30};
for (int v : values) {
    System.out.println(v);
}
```

### while

```java
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}
```

### do-while

```java
int n;
do {
    n = scanner.nextInt();
} while (n != 0);
```

### break and continue

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;    // skip 3
    if (i == 7) break;       // stop at 7
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

### Reading a known count

```java
int n = scanner.nextInt();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    int x = scanner.nextInt();
    // process x
}
```

### Sentinel loop

```java
while (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
    int n = scanner.nextInt();
    if (n == -1) break;
    sum += n;
}
```

---

**Next up:** loop patterns — accumulation, counting, and min/max.

## Loop Patterns: Accumulation, Counting & Min/Max

### Accumulation

```java
int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    total += i;
}
```

### Counting

```java
int evens = 0;
for (int v : values) {
    if (v % 2 == 0) evens++;
}
```

### Min/max tracking

```java
int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
while (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
    int n = scanner.nextInt();
    min = Math.min(min, n);
    max = Math.max(max, n);
}
```

### Flag pattern

```java
boolean found = false;
for (int v : values) {
    if (v == target) { found = true; break; }
}
System.out.println(found ? "found" : "missing");
```

### Sum of digits

```java
int n = 1234, sum = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    sum += n % 10;   // extract last digit
    n /= 10;         // drop last digit
}
```

### Reversing a number

```java
int rev = 0;
while (n > 0) {
    rev = rev * 10 + n % 10;
    n /= 10;
}
```

---

**Next up:** nested loops and pattern printing.

## Nested Loops and Pattern Printing

### Nested loops

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        System.out.print("(" + i + "," + j + ") ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

### Triangle of stars

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        System.out.print("*");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

### Multiplication table

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
        System.out.printf("%4d", i * j);
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

### Labelled break

You can break out of nested loops with a label:

```java
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
        if (i * j == 12) break outer;
    }
}
```

Use labels sparingly — a boolean flag is often clearer.

### Prime check

```java
boolean isPrime = n >= 2;
for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
    if (n % i == 0) { isPrime = false; break; }
}
```

