## Classes, Objects & Methods

A **class** is a blueprint; an **object** is an instance created from that blueprint.

### Defining a class

```java
public class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    void introduce() {
        System.out.println("Hi, I'm " + name + ", age " + age);
    }
}
```

### Creating and using objects

```java
Student alice = new Student();   // new allocates and calls the constructor
alice.name = "Alice";
alice.age = 20;
alice.introduce();
```

### Constructors

```java
public class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    public Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;   // this disambiguates from the parameter
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

```java
Student bob = new Student("Bob", 22);
```

### Method overloading

Methods can share a name if their parameter lists differ:

```java
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int add(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; }
```

### The this keyword

`this` refers to the current object — needed when a parameter shadows a field.

---

**Next up:** encapsulation and access modifiers.

## Encapsulation: private, getters & setters

**Encapsulation** hides an object's internal state and exposes controlled access.

### Access modifiers

| Modifier   | Same class | Same package | Subclass | Anywhere |
|------------|-----------|--------------|----------|----------|
| `private`  | yes       | no           | no       | no       |
| (none)     | yes       | yes          | yes      | no       |
| `protected`| yes       | yes          | yes      | no       |
| `public`   | yes       | yes          | yes      | yes      |

### Getter and setter pattern

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    public boolean withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance) {
            balance -= amount;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

### Why encapsulate

- You can validate data in setters.
- You can change the internal representation without breaking callers.
- You control who reads and who writes.

### Immutable classes

```java
public final class Point {
    private final int x;
    private final int y;

    public Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public int getX() { return x; }
    public int getY() { return y; }
}
```

---

**Next up:** inheritance and the `super` keyword.

## Inheritance and super

**Inheritance** lets a class reuse and extend another class's behavior.

### Extending a class

```java
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void speak() {
        System.out.println("...");
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);              // call the parent constructor
    }

    @Override
    public void speak() {
        System.out.println(name + " says Woof!");
    }
}
```

### The super keyword

- `super(...)` calls the parent constructor — must be the first statement.
- `super.method()` calls a parent method explicitly.

### @Override

Marking a method `@Override` lets the compiler catch mistakes where you intended to override but wrote a different signature.

### What is inherited

- `public` and `protected` members.
- `private` members are **not** inherited.
- Constructors are not inherited.

### Method overriding rules

The overriding method must have the same signature and cannot reduce visibility.

### Is-a relationship

`Dog` is an `Animal`. Inheritance models "is-a", composition models "has-a".

---

**Next up:** polymorphism and interfaces.

## Polymorphism and Interfaces

**Polymorphism** lets one reference behave differently based on the actual object type.

### Upcasting

```java
Animal pet = new Dog("Rex");
pet.speak();    // runtime dispatch → "Rex says Woof!"
```

The variable type is `Animal`, but the method called is `Dog.speak()` — this is **dynamic dispatch**.

### Interfaces

An interface declares a contract; classes implement it:

```java
public interface Drawable {
    void draw();
}

public class Circle implements Drawable {
    @Override
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a circle");
    }
}

public class Square implements Drawable {
    @Override
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a square");
    }
}
```

### Programming to the interface

```java
Drawable shape = new Circle();
shape.draw();          // which draw() runs depends on the object

Drawable[] shapes = {new Circle(), new Square()};
for (Drawable s : shapes) {
    s.draw();
}
```

### Default methods (Java 8+)

```java
public interface Greeter {
    void greet(String name);

    default void greetFormally(String name) {
        System.out.println("Good day, " + name);
    }
}
```

### Abstract classes vs interfaces

- Abstract class: shared **state** + behavior; single inheritance.
- Interface: contract only (plus defaults); a class can implement many.

