## File Reading and Writing

Java reads files through reader classes layered over streams.

### Reading a file line by line

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    String line;
    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
        System.out.println(line);
    }
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.err.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

The `try`-with-resources block closes the file automatically.

### Reading with Files helper (Java 11+)

```java
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;

String content = Files.readString(Path.of("data.txt"));
List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(Path.of("data.txt"));
```

### Writing a file

```java
import java.io.FileWriter;

try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("out.txt")) {
    writer.write("line one\n");
    writer.write("line two\n");
}
```

`FileWriter` overwrites by default; use the append constructor to add:

```java
new FileWriter("out.txt", true)   // true = append
```

### Writing with Files

```java
Files.writeString(Path.of("out.txt"), "Hello\n");
```

### Scanner over a file

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(new java.io.File("data.txt"));
while (sc.hasNextLine()) {
    System.out.println(sc.nextLine());
}
```

---

**Next up:** generics — type-safe reusable code.

## Generic Classes and Methods

Generics let you write code that works with any type while keeping compile-time safety.

### A generic class

```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T value;

    public void set(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public T get() {
        return value;
    }
}
```

```java
Box<Integer> intBox = new Box<>();
intBox.set(42);

Box<String> strBox = new Box<>();
strBox.set("hello");
```

### A generic method

```java
public static <T> T first(T[] items) {
    return items[0];
}
```

### Bounded type parameters

Restrict `T` to subtypes of a bound:

```java
public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(T a, T b) {
    return a.compareTo(b) >= 0 ? a : b;
}
```

### Wildcards

- `List<?>` — a list of some unknown type (read-only).
- `List<? extends Number>` — numbers or a subclass.
- `List<? super Integer>` — Integer or a supertype.

### Why generics matter

- Compile-time type checking.
- No manual casting.
- Reusable collections and algorithms.

---

